--
-- MIGRATION CORRECTION POLICY (Decision 229): this project's own
-- established convention — "migrations are immutable once applied;
-- corrections are always new migrations" (Decision 218) — is scoped
-- explicitly to APPLIED migrations. Migrations 152-154 have never been
-- applied to any database and have never been independently reviewed
-- or Founder-approved; there is no production-application record to
-- preserve. Decision 229 therefore corrects this migration's content
-- directly in place, rather than authoring a new 155/156/157 corrective
-- set, avoiding a confusing multi-migration chain for content that was
-- never once applied. This does not change or reinterpret the
-- immutability rule for already-applied migrations, which continues to
-- apply without exception.
--
-- FACTUAL VERIFICATION CONTROL (Decision 229) — new standing
-- convention for Angel-authored non-fiction/informational passages:
-- automated tests prove internal consistency (answers match stored
-- passage text) but cannot, by themselves, prove a real-world factual
-- claim is true. From this migration forward, any Angel-authored
-- passage with text_type = 'informational' presenting real-world
-- factual claims (science/history/geography/etc.) must carry, in its
-- own migration header, a FACTUAL VERIFICATION disclosure for each
-- such claim, tagged:
--   SOURCE-CONTAINS        — the claim as stated in an independently
--                             identified, authoritative source.
--   ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION    — how Angel's own original passage prose
--                             simplifies or reframes that source claim
--                             for a 10-11-year-old reader.
--   FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE      — HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW, reflecting source
--                             agreement and the claim's own certainty.
--   UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS — named explicitly if any exist; "None
--                             identified" otherwise.
-- This is a documentation-and-test convention only, not a new approval
-- gate or governance body — it does not change eligibility_status
-- transitions, review workflow, or any existing sign-off requirement.
-- Verification evidence for this migration's own bee-navigation
-- passage:
--   Claim: Karl von Frisch published his full decoding of the waggle
--   dance's meaning in 1946 ("Die Tänze der Bienen"), work for which he
--   later received the Nobel Prize.
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: multiple independent, authoritative sources
--       (Springer/Insectes Sociaux "The dance legacy of Karl von
--       Frisch"; Bee Craft "How Karl von Frisch deciphered the waggle
--       dance"; EBSCO Research Starters "Frisch Discovers Bees
--       Communicate Through Body Movements") describe von Frisch's full
--       published account of the waggle dance's meaning as 1946
--       ("Die Tänze der Bienen"), following an initial 1927 description
--       of bee communication, with the Nobel Prize in Physiology or
--       Medicine awarded in 1973 for this body of work.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage names von Frisch and the 1946
--       publication year as a single, clean retrieval fact, and
--       mentions the Nobel Prize only briefly, without a specific year,
--       to avoid overloading a 10-11-year-old reader with multiple
--       memorisable dates.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH (multiple independent, authoritative,
--       mutually-consistent sources).
--     UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: None identified.
--   Claim: honeybees show real evidence of sensitivity to the Earth's
--   magnetic field, via iron-rich particles in the abdomen, but the
--   extent of its role in everyday navigation remains genuinely
--   uncertain/under investigation.
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: peer-reviewed sources (Springer/Animal Cognition
--       and PMC, "Magnetoreception in Hymenoptera: importance for
--       navigation"; Nature Scientific Reports, "Magnetic Sensing
--       through the Abdomen of the Honey Bee") document iron-rich
--       granules (magnetite) in abdominal trophocytes, experiments
--       showing magnetising a bee's abdomen disrupts local-magnetic-
--       field-based navigation, and explicitly describe magnetoreception
--       as "one of the least understood senses" in honeybees, with open
--       scientific debate over whether these granules are the true
--       magnetoreceptor organ.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage states the real evidence (iron-
--       rich particles, interference affecting navigation) without
--       naming trophocytes/magnetite, and explicitly flags that its
--       contribution to everyday navigation is still being
--       investigated, deliberately not presenting it as a confirmed,
--       equally-established third system alongside the sun-compass and
--       landmark-memory systems.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: MEDIUM (sensitivity itself is well evidenced;
--       its precise navigational role and mechanism remain genuinely
--       open questions in the cited literature).
--     UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: whether abdominal iron granules are
--       the actual magnetoreceptor organ, versus an alternative
--       location (e.g. the antennae) or a non-sensory function (iron
--       storage), is explicitly unresolved in the cited literature —
--       the corrected passage avoids asserting a specific mechanism for
--       this reason.
--
